    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> hold that there is one God, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">YHWH</a>, that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> is the only true holy book, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>, not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, is the place God chose.</li><li>Scholars see Samaritanism as taking shape as a distinct tradition in the Hasmonean and Roman era, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a> destroyed the Gerizim temple around 120 BCE; Samaritan tradition traces the separation to a schism in the time of the High Priest Eli.</li><li>About 900 registered communal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> remain in 2025; they take this small number to be the fulfilment of a scriptural prophecy and hope for the Taheb, the Restorer.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> hold that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binding-of-isaac">Binding of Isaac</a> took place on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>, not at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>'s Temple Mount, and in their version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-commandments">Ten Commandments</a> the tenth commands an altar to be built on Gerizim.</p></aside>
    </section>

