    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Samaritanism holds itself to be the truly preserved form of the monotheistic faith that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> kept under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. Samaritan historiography traces the separation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to a period soon after the Israelites entered the Promised Land. In this account the High Priest Eli left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>, where stood the first Israelite altar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, and built a competing altar at Shiloh. Those who followed him later settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and became the Jews, while those who stayed on Gerizim, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, became the Samaritans. The 14th-century historian Abu'l-Fath tells of a civil war between Eli and the sons of Phinehas, and the Samaritan New Chronicle, believed to have been composed in the 18th century, says the Israelites divided into three groups in Eli's time.</p><p>Scholars give a different account. A genetic study of 2004 suggests that Samaritan lineages trace back to a common ancestor with Jews in the paternally inherited priesthood, close in time to the Assyrian conquest of the kingdom of Israel, and that the Samaritans are probably descendants of the historical Israelite population. The proto-Samaritans' religion was probably no different from that of Judea, and Samaritanism likely did not emerge as a distinct tradition until the Hasmonean and Roman era. The temple on Mount Gerizim was built in the 5th century BCE as one of many Yahwistic temples in Samaria, and a large building programme began around the 4th century BCE. Debates between Jews and Samaritans are attested from the 2nd century BCE.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/torah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torah.jpg" alt="Torah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Тора._Нацыянальная_бібліятэка_Беларусі.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> believe in one God, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">YHWH</a>, the same God recognised by the Jewish prophets. They hold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> to be the only true holy book, given by God to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, whom they consider the final prophet; after him the only authority is the successive Samaritan priesthood, from the line of Aaron. For Samaritans <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a>, not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, is the one sanctuary chosen by God, and they do not recognise the sanctity of Jerusalem or the Temple Mount. They believe that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a> was where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binding-of-isaac">Binding of Isaac</a> took place. They also expect an apocalypse, the day of vengeance, at the end of days.</p><p>Samaritans hope for the Taheb, the Restorer, a figure from the tribe of Joseph. In their belief he will be a prophet like Moses for 40 years and bring back the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a>; the dead will be resurrected, and he will discover the Tabernacle, bring it to Mount Gerizim and be buried next to Joseph. They hold that he will perform three signs and that Gerizim will be restored to its former glory.</p><p>The Samaritans keep the sabbath from Friday to Saturday sundown with seven prayer services, and they celebrate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/passover">Pesach</a>, Shavuot and Sukkot by their own method of fixing the dates. Passover is particularly important and climaxes with the sacrifice of up to 40 sheep. During festivals the liturgy is recited with a preserved Samaritan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> pronunciation of the Tetragrammaton.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/talmud"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/talmud.jpg" alt="Talmud" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Talmud-Druck_von_Daniel_Bomberg_und_Ambrosius_Froben.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LGLou, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, in its Samaritan version, is the only scripture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> accept; they reject the other books of the Tanakh and all later Jewish literature, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a>, the Midrash and the Tosefta. They have a variation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-joshua">Book of Joshua</a>, but do not regard it as scripture. There are some 6,000 differences between the Samaritan Pentateuch and the Masoretic Jewish text, and by one estimate 1,900 points of agreement with the Greek LXX version. The variations, some corroborated by readings in the Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and Ethiopian translations, attest to the antiquity of the Samaritan text, although the exact date of its composition is still largely unclear. The Abisha Scroll is attributed to Abishua, grandson of Aaron, and traditionally compiled during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>.</p><p>The most important differences concern the status of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>. In the Samaritan version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-commandments">Ten Commandments</a> the tenth commands an altar to be built for God on Gerizim, and the Samaritan Pentateuch speaks of the place God has chosen, where the Jewish Torah has the place God will choose.</p><p>Samaritan law differs from rabbinic halakha. Historical writings include the Tolidah, the Chronicle of Joshua, the Adler chronicle and the Kitab al-Tarikh of Abu'l-Fath. Halakhic texts include the Hillukh and the 12th-century Kitab at-Tabbah. The Memar Markah, theological treatises attributed to Hakkam Markha, is dated to the 3rd or 4th century, and the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> Al-Asatir to the 11th and 12th.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Samaritanism is an ethnic religion, the collective spiritual, cultural and legal tradition of the Samaritan people, who often prefer to be called Israelite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a>. Its centre is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaria">Samaria</a>, which Samaritans revere as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>' original holy site. The temple there stood until the Hasmonean ruler and high priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hyrcanus">John Hyrcanus</a> destroyed it and brought Samaria under his control around 120 BCE.</p><p>That destruction led to a lasting sense of mutual hostility between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Samaritans. From this point the Samaritans likely sought to distance themselves consciously from their Judean kin, and both peoples came to see the Samaritan faith as a religion distinct from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>. The relationship deteriorated with time, and by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> Samaritans and Jews deeply disparaged one another, as the Parable of the Good Samaritan shows.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> persecuted the Samaritans, practically annihilating them after the Samaritan revolts of the 5th and 6th centuries. The Samaritan historian Benyamim Tsedaka traces the Samaritan custom of building the sukkah inside houses to persecution under the Byzantine Empire. In the present day the community has its own local priest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holon">Holon</a> in Israel, and in 2025 there are approximately 900 registered communal Samaritans.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The highest position in Samaritanism is the High Priest, chosen from the priestly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, which is held to descend from the house of Aaron, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>. His residence is on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a>, and since 1624 the oldest member of the priestly family has been chosen for the position. After him come the other priests, who usually wear a red turban and cassock, changing to more striking clothing on Saturdays and other sacred days. The community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holon">Holon</a> has its own local priest.</p><p>Samaritan custom prescribes that members of the priestly family do not work but deal only with religious matters, and that the rest of the community supports them through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> and offerings. In 2003 the priestly family made up 28 percent of the community, so this arrangement has become impractical and its members now seek work like other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a>. From the priestly family come not only the priests but also the leaders of prayers (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>) and the rabbis, who teach the ancient Samaritan language. Samaritan synagogues are oriented toward Gerizim and usually stand on the outskirts of settlements.</p><p>Samaritans are not usually recognised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> by Orthodox Jews, especially rabbis, a rejection based on the accounts in the Books of Kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> around 720 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Babylonian Talmud</a> says a Samaritan converting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> must first renounce any belief in the sanctity of Gerizim, and that Samaritans should be treated as Jews where their practices are the same and as gentiles where they differ. Since the 19th century many Jews have considered Samaritans a Jewish sect.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> preserve the Parable of the Good Samaritan and other allusions showing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> did not associate with one another. Such stories refer to the relationship between Samaritanism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, not to the relationship between Samaritanism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Relations with Christianity do not appear to have been entirely tolerant in the first Christian centuries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> persecuted the Samaritans, practically annihilating them after the revolts of the 5th and 6th centuries.</p><p>Samaritanism remains a living tradition of about 900 registered communal Samaritans in 2025. Samaritans believe that this small population is a prophecy fulfilled, from a scriptural passage that says, “You will be left few in number.” Their faith keeps its own priesthood, its own sabbath and festival practice, and its own version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, which they regard as the true preserved form of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>' faith.</p><p>Samaritans hope for a future time when the Taheb will perform three signs, an era of divine favour will return, the hidden tabernacle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> will be miraculously revealed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a> will be restored to its former glory. Jews and Samaritans each hold that their own people kept the original faith of the Israelites, and each holds a different place to be the site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/binding-of-isaac">Binding of Isaac</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Samaritanism is the ethnic religion of the Samaritan people, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a> and the Samaritan Pentateuch. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> hold it to be the faith the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> kept under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>; scholars see a distinct tradition emerging in the Hasmonean and Roman era. About 900 registered communal Samaritans remain in 2025.</em></p>

